<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <ScenTitle>No BREXIT, no Problem!</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;&lt;B style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal"&gt;Scenario 2: No BREXIT, no Problem!&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = "o" ns = "urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;&lt;B style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal"&gt;Scenario Introduction&lt;/B&gt;: &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;23 August 2016: Russia is feeling frustrated and pressured to act by the continual provocations of NATO flexing its muscles. If something is not done quickly, an opportunity to destroy NATO’s credibility will be missed.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;Eastern Europe must be shown that collective defence is a ‘chimera’ and that NATO has no teeth!&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;Over the past several months Europe has grown more vociferous in its call to curb Russian imperialism in Eastern Europe.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;Critical analysis shows that much of the political ‘table pounding’ is more related to diverting public attention from the growing refugee crisis as hundreds of thousands arrive in a Europe with no viable plan to cope with them. Although the British decision not to leave the EU has strengthened that body politically, the Euro is being marginalized by the US dollar, and a resurgent British Pound; economic stagnation is a real possibility.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;Meanwhile the US is going through one of the most divisive and violent election campaigns in history, with dozens if not hundreds of protesters arrested at every political event and where the number of politically motivated killings is shocking both the world and the domestic political establishment.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;NATO decision makers have struggled with a patchwork of collective defence initiatives in the Baltic and Ukraine. As reinforcement to the standing ‘Baltic Air Policing’ operation and training missions in the Baltic countries and Ukraine; a multi-national Brigade, led by four nations and consisting of troops from 5 others, spread across four countries (Latvia, Lithuania, Estonia &amp;amp; Poland) is touted as the solution. Although judged anemic by most experts, placing American, British, German and Canadian troops in the Baltics displays a commitment to the region that if Russia were to attack it would be attacking the whole alliance not just the local forces.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;The Russian Federation however, desires an environment more open to resurgent influence from Moscow. To achieve this, the whole idea of NATO’s collective defence through a cohesive command structure with an ability to react rapidly must be put into question.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;The Baltic States (Latvia, Lithuania, and Estonia) have long been a thorn in the side of the Russian Federation: Ethnically they have large Russian populations; strategically they isolate the key to Baltic defence, Kaliningrad; politically they should never have been allowed to slip into NATO’s sphere of influence.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;Perhaps now is an ideal opportunity to right the wrongs of the past and exert newfound strength before NATO can put a multi-national ‘trip wire’ in front of them. If the Baltic States can be taken in a short 1-2 day campaign without tipping the world into another global conflict, NATO may never recover.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;A short window of opportunity exists before the Multi-National Brigade deploys, if only a few aircraft are engaged and no ground troops, it is much easier to de-escalate.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;&lt;B style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal"&gt;Designers Note&lt;/B&gt;:&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;This scenario is a counterpoint to ‘&lt;B style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal"&gt;You BREXIT, You Fix it!’ &lt;/B&gt;and portrays a cohesive NATO response.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;Although perhaps unlikely, the scenario portrays a plausible ‘what if’ situation.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;You can expect NATO forces to start quite weak and dispersed but as time and events occur become much stronger; NATO response will be more coherent and rapid than the ‘Post BREXIT’ version.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;The North Atlantic Council (NAC) will undoubtedly convene within a very short time, and political consensus on a response will be more decisive.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;The non-NATO countries of Sweden and Finland are still a factor and may intervene with or even before NATO.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P class=MsoNormal style="MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 10pt"&gt;Russia is walking a very fine line; its forces are limited to those that are ‘in-situ’ in the region so as not to tip-off NATO planners.&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;Reinforcements may be added but only reluctantly, Russia is not interested in general war, only the limited operational objective of seizing the Baltic States (or re-establishing status quo, in some views).&lt;SPAN style="mso-spacerun: yes"&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/SPAN&gt;Strategically the objective is to destabilize NATO; victory will crush the view that Collective Defence will protect countries in Eastern Europe. Causing NATO to react cohesively however will have the opposite effect.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
